/* ================= Practice Test 2 · Reading and Writing ================= */
testSet('t2', 'RW', 'm1', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'After climbing for six hours, the hikers finally ______ the summit just before sunset.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['reached', 'avoided', 'designed', 'forgot'], a: 0, x: 'Arriving at the top after climbing means they **reached** the summit.', w: ['', 'They climbed for six hours toward the summit, so they didn’t avoid it.', 'Hikers don’t design a summit.', '“Forgot the summit” makes no sense after hours of climbing toward it.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The witness’s account was ______: she changed important details each time she told the story, so investigators were reluctant to rely on it.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['vivid', 'consistent', 'unreliable', 'brief'], a: 2, x: 'Changing details each time makes an account **unreliable**. “Consistent” is the opposite.', w: ['A vivid account could still be trusted; the problem is that the details kept changing.', 'Changing details each time is the opposite of consistent.', '', 'Length isn’t the issue; the details kept changing.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The novelist’s early work was **marked** by long, winding sentences; her later books favor short, direct ones.', p: Q_MEAN('marked'), o: ['graded', 'characterized', 'damaged', 'announced'], a: 1, x: 'Long, winding sentences were a typical feature of her early work, so “marked by” means **characterized** by.', w: ['“Graded” is a school meaning of “marked”; it doesn’t fit a style of writing.', '', 'Long sentences didn’t damage her work; they were a typical feature of it.', 'Her work didn’t announce long sentences; it was known for them.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Far from being ______, the engineer’s proposal was grounded in years of field measurements and careful calculation.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['rigorous', 'costly', 'popular', 'speculative'], a: 3, x: '“Far from being X” sets up the opposite of “grounded in measurements”: **speculative** (based on guesses).', w: ['“Far from being” needs the opposite of careful work, but “rigorous” means careful.', 'Cost has nothing to do with being grounded in measurements.', 'Popularity isn’t the opposite of being grounded in measurements.', ''] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'Each fall, monarch butterflies fly thousands of kilometers from Canada and the United States to forests in central Mexico. ==No single butterfly completes the round trip, however: the monarchs that reach Canada the following summer are the grandchildren or great-grandchildren of those that flew south.== Scientists are still studying how each new generation finds its way along a route it has never traveled.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It presents a surprising fact about the migration that leads to the question described in the next sentence.', 'It explains why monarchs spend the winter in Mexico rather than somewhere else.', 'It describes the forests where monarchs spend the winter.', 'It challenges the claim that monarchs travel long distances.'], a: 0, x: 'The underlined fact (no butterfly makes the whole trip) is what makes the next sentence’s puzzle possible: how do new generations know the way?', w: ['', 'The sentence doesn’t explain why they winter in Mexico.', 'The forests are mentioned in the first sentence, not described in the underlined one.', 'The text accepts that monarchs travel thousands of kilometers.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'In the 1840s, the Hungarian doctor Ignaz Semmelweis found that deaths among new mothers in his hospital fell sharply after doctors began washing their hands with a chlorine solution. Yet many of his colleagues rejected his findings, partly because he could not explain why handwashing worked: the germ theory of disease had not yet been established.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To argue that modern doctors should wash their hands more often', 'To explain how the germ theory of disease was developed', 'To criticize Semmelweis for keeping his results secret', 'To describe a medical discovery and explain why it was initially resisted'], a: 3, x: 'The text presents Semmelweis’s finding and then explains why colleagues rejected it.', w: ['The text describes the 1840s and doesn’t give advice to modern doctors.', 'Germ theory is mentioned only as something not yet established; its development isn’t explained.', 'The text doesn’t say he kept his results secret; his colleagues rejected them.', ''] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nSome linguists argue that the language people speak shapes how they perceive the world. For example, speakers of a language with more basic color words should be better at telling colors apart.\n\n**Text 2**\nIn experiments, Russian speakers, whose language has separate basic words for light blue and dark blue, were slightly faster than English speakers at telling those shades apart. But the advantage disappeared when participants were given a task that kept them from silently naming the colors. This suggests that language affects perception mainly by supplying words in the moment, not by permanently changing how people see.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the linguists in Text 1?', o: ['By rejecting any connection between language and perception', 'By agreeing that language can affect color perception but suggesting that the effect depends on using words during the task', 'By arguing that English speakers perceive colors more accurately than Russian speakers do', 'By claiming that Russian has fewer color words than English does'], a: 1, x: 'Text 2 accepts that Russian speakers had an advantage, but shows it vanished when they couldn’t name colors, so the effect is temporary, not a lasting change in how they see.', w: ['Text 2 found an effect while people could name the colors, so it doesn’t reject any connection.', '', 'Russian speakers were faster at telling the blues apart, not English speakers.', 'Text 2 says Russian has separate words for light and dark blue, which English doesn’t.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'Archaeologists have reported finding pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that had not spoiled after thousands of years. Honey resists spoiling because it contains very little water and is slightly acidic, conditions in which most bacteria cannot grow.', p: 'According to the text, why does honey resist spoiling?', o: ['It was stored in sealed tombs.', 'It contains little water and is slightly acidic, so most bacteria cannot grow in it.', 'It is usually kept cold.', 'Ancient Egyptians added salt to it.'], a: 1, x: 'The text gives the reason directly: little water and slight acidity stop most bacteria from growing. The tombs are just where it was found.', w: ['The tombs are only where the honey was found; the reason is its low water and acidity.', '', 'Temperature isn’t mentioned.', 'Salt isn’t mentioned.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'The old ferryman had crossed the river so many times that he no longer looked at the water. He steered by the sound of the current against the boat, by the smell of the reeds near the far bank, and by a feeling in his arms that told him when the boat had begun to drift.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['The ferryman is losing his eyesight.', 'The ferryman knows the river so well that he can steer without looking at it.', 'The river is too dangerous to cross at night.', 'The ferryman dislikes his job.'], a: 1, x: 'He steers by sound, smell and feel because he has crossed so many times. Nothing says his eyesight is failing.', w: ['Nothing says his eyesight is failing; he doesn’t need to look because he knows the river.', '', 'Nothing mentions night or danger.', 'Nothing suggests he dislikes the work; he’s simply skilled at it.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'In a poem, the speaker suggests that memories of childhood remain vivid even after many years.', p: 'Which quotation from the poem most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“The house was sold the year I turned sixteen.”', '“My brother and I shared a narrow room.”', '“Forty winters since, and still I smell / the bread my mother baked on Sunday mornings.”', '“The town has changed; the bakery is a bank.”'], a: 2, x: 'Still smelling the bread after forty winters shows a memory that stays vivid.', w: ['Selling the house is a fact, not a vivid memory.', 'Sharing a room is a fact without any sense of the memory staying vivid.', '', 'This describes how the town changed, not a vivid memory.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'A researcher claims that sleep helps people remember newly learned physical skills.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher’s claim?', o: ['Participants reported feeling tired after learning a finger-tapping sequence.', 'Most adults sleep about seven hours a night.', 'Participants who slept after learning a finger-tapping sequence and those who stayed awake performed equally well 12 hours later.', 'Participants who learned a finger-tapping sequence in the evening and then slept performed it more accurately 12 hours later than participants who learned it in the morning and stayed awake.'], a: 3, x: 'Comparing people who slept with people who stayed awake isolates the effect of sleep, and the sleepers did better.', w: ['Feeling tired doesn’t show whether sleep improved memory of the skill.', 'Typical sleep length doesn’t test the claim.', 'Equal performance would weaken the claim, not support it.', ''] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 3, ps: 'A student compared four cities and claims that in cities where a monthly transit pass is cheaper, a larger share of commuters use public transit.', ch: { t: 'table', title: 'Transit pass prices and use', head: ['City', 'Monthly pass (dollars)', 'Commuters using transit'], rows: [['Alton', '40', '58%'], ['Brook', '65', '41%'], ['Corley', '90', '27%'], ['Dunmore', '110', '19%']] }, p: 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support the student’s claim?', o: ['A monthly pass in Brook costs 65 dollars.', 'Dunmore has the most expensive pass and the lowest share of transit users (19%), while Alton has the cheapest pass and the highest share (58%).', 'Corley’s pass costs more than Brook’s, and 27% of commuters in Corley use transit.', 'In Alton, 58% of commuters use transit.'], a: 1, x: 'The claim links price and transit use, so the answer must compare both across cities. Only one choice does.', w: ['This gives one city’s price, without comparing transit use.', '', 'This compares two cities only partly and doesn’t show the full pattern.', 'This gives one city’s transit share, without comparing prices.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'Researchers placed two identical bowls of seed in a garden, one in the open and one under a thick bush. Small birds fed mostly from the bowl under the bush, even though they had to crowd together there. When the researchers set a model hawk on a nearby post, the birds stopped visiting the open bowl entirely. This suggests that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the seed under the bush tasted better than the seed in the open.', 'the birds were choosing the bush partly because it offered cover from predators.', 'the birds were not afraid of hawks.', 'small birds prefer to feed in large open spaces.'], a: 1, x: 'The food was identical, and a hawk made the birds avoid the open entirely, so cover from predators explains their choice.', w: ['The bowls held identical seed.', '', 'The birds stopped using the open bowl when the hawk appeared, so they were afraid.', 'The birds mostly chose the covered bowl, not the open one.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'Ancient Roman concrete in harbor walls has survived 2,000 years of seawater, while modern concrete in seawater often cracks within decades. Researchers found that seawater seeping into the Roman concrete reacted with volcanic ash in the mix, forming new minerals that filled cracks as they appeared. This suggests that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the volcanic ash helped Roman concrete repair itself over time.', 'modern concrete contains too much volcanic ash.', 'Roman builders tried to keep seawater away from their concrete.', 'seawater damages all kinds of concrete equally.'], a: 0, x: 'Minerals that fill cracks as they form act as a kind of self-repair, made possible by the ash.', w: ['', 'The text says nothing about how much ash modern concrete contains.', 'The seawater seeping in is what made the repairs happen.', 'Roman concrete survived while modern concrete cracks, so the damage isn’t equal.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'Owls can turn their heads about 270 ______ they have more neck bones than most other birds.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['degrees because', 'degrees; because', 'degrees, because,', 'degrees: because'], a: 0, x: '“Because they have…” is a dependent clause, so no punctuation is needed before it.', w: ['', 'A semicolon can’t come before “because”, since “because they have…” isn’t a complete sentence.', 'No comma belongs after “because”.', 'A colon can’t come before “because they have…”, which isn’t a complete sentence.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The town’s most famous ______ a 300-year-old oak tree, draws visitors from across the region.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['landmark', 'landmark;', 'landmark,', 'landmark:'], a: 2, x: '“A 300-year-old oak tree” renames the landmark and is closed by a comma, so it must also open with one.', w: ['The description closes with a comma, so it needs an opening comma too.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “draws”.', '', 'A colon would cut the subject off from its verb, “draws”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The researchers who first described the deep-sea ______ named it after the ship that carried them.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['creature,', 'creature', 'creature;', 'creature—'], a: 1, x: 'The subject is “the researchers who first described the deep-sea creature”, and no punctuation goes between a subject and its verb (“named”).', w: ['A comma here separates the subject from its verb, “named”.', '', 'A semicolon here separates the subject from its verb.', 'A dash here separates the subject from its verb.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The first trial of the new drug was ______ the second trial, which included far more patients, showed no clear benefit.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['promising, however,', 'promising however', 'promising; however', 'promising; however,'], a: 3, x: 'Two complete sentences joined by “however” need a semicolon before it and a comma after it.', w: ['Commas around “however” can’t join two complete sentences; that’s a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, the two sentences run together.', '“However” at the start of a clause needs a comma after it.', ''] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'The flowers along the garden path ______ earlier than usual this year.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['has bloomed', 'have bloomed', 'is blooming', 'was blooming'], a: 1, x: 'The subject is “flowers” (plural), so “have bloomed”.', w: ['“Has” is singular, but “flowers” is plural.', '', '“Is” is singular, but “flowers” is plural.', '“Was” is singular, but “flowers” is plural.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'When the students finished the experiment, the teacher collected ______ notebooks and graded them over the weekend.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['its', 'their', 'they’re', 'there'], a: 1, x: 'The notebooks belong to the students (plural), so “their”.', w: ['“Its” is singular, but “students” is plural.', '', '“They’re” means “they are”.', '“There” refers to a place.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'The list of ingredients that the chef posted on the restaurant’s website ______ several items that are hard to find outside the region.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['include', 'are including', 'includes', 'have included'], a: 2, x: 'The subject is “the list” (singular), not “ingredients” or “chef”, so “includes”.', w: ['The subject is “the list”, which is singular.', '“Are including” is plural, but “the list” is singular.', '', '“Have included” is plural, but “the list” is singular.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'The first bicycles had no pedals. ______ riders pushed themselves along with their feet.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Instead,', 'Therefore,', 'For example,', 'Similarly,'], a: 0, x: 'Pushing with their feet was what riders did in place of pedaling: “Instead”.', w: ['', '“Therefore” would present this as a conclusion, but the sentence describes what riders did in place of pedaling.', 'Pushing with their feet isn’t an example of having no pedals.', 'Pushing with their feet isn’t similar to having no pedals.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'Botanically, the tomato is a fruit, because it develops from a flower and contains seeds. ______ in 1893 the U.S. Supreme Court ruled that tomatoes should be taxed as vegetables, since people usually eat them as part of a meal rather than as dessert.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Therefore,', 'In addition,', 'Nevertheless,', 'For instance,'], a: 2, x: 'The court treated tomatoes as vegetables despite their being fruits botanically: a contrast.', w: ['Being taxed as a vegetable isn’t a result of being a fruit; it’s the opposite.', 'The ruling doesn’t add to the first point; it goes against it.', '', 'The ruling isn’t an example of the tomato being a fruit.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Many early photographs show people with stiff, serious expressions. This was partly a practical matter: exposures could last several seconds, and a smile is hard to hold. ______ many people thought of photographs as formal records, like painted portraits, rather than snapshots of everyday life.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Instead,', 'Moreover,', 'Nonetheless,', 'In other words,'], a: 1, x: 'The last sentence adds a second reason for the serious expressions: “Moreover”.', w: ['“Instead” would mean replacing the first reason, but the sentence adds a second one.', '', '“Nonetheless” signals a contrast, but the sentence adds another reason in the same direction.', 'The sentence gives a new reason; it doesn’t restate the first.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- The Mariana Trench is in the western Pacific Ocean.\n- Its deepest point is called the Challenger Deep.\n- The Challenger Deep is about 11,000 meters below sea level.\n- In 1960, two explorers reached the bottom in a vessel called the *Trieste*.', p: 'The student wants to state how deep the trench’s deepest point is. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The Mariana Trench is in the western Pacific Ocean.', 'In 1960, two explorers reached the bottom of the trench.', 'The *Trieste* carried explorers into the Mariana Trench.', 'The Challenger Deep, the deepest point of the Mariana Trench, lies about 11,000 meters below sea level.'], a: 3, x: 'Only one choice gives the depth of the deepest point.', w: ['This gives the location, not the depth.', 'This gives a date of exploration, not the depth.', 'This names the vessel, not the depth.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Frogs and toads are both amphibians.\n- Frogs have smooth, moist skin; toads have dry, bumpy skin.\n- Frogs usually live in or near water.\n- Toads can live much farther from water.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize a difference in where frogs and toads live. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Frogs have smooth, moist skin, while toads have dry, bumpy skin.', 'Frogs and toads are both amphibians.', 'While frogs usually live in or near water, toads can live much farther from it.', 'Frogs usually live in or near water.'], a: 2, x: 'The goal is a difference in **where** they live. The skin difference is a real difference, but the wrong one.', w: ['This is a real difference, but it’s about skin, not where they live.', 'This is a similarity, not a difference.', '', 'This gives only where frogs live, so nothing is contrasted.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- Florence Price (1887–1953) was an American composer.\n- In 1933, the Chicago Symphony Orchestra performed her Symphony in E minor.\n- It was the first symphony by a Black woman composer to be performed by a major American orchestra.\n- Many of her manuscripts were rediscovered in an abandoned house in 2009.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize the historical significance of the 1933 performance. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['In 1933, Price’s Symphony in E minor became the first symphony by a Black woman composer to be performed by a major American orchestra.', 'Florence Price, an American composer, lived from 1887 to 1953.', 'Many of Price’s manuscripts were rediscovered in an abandoned house in 2009.', 'The Chicago Symphony Orchestra performed a symphony by Florence Price in 1933.'], a: 0, x: 'Saying only that the orchestra performed her symphony in 1933 doesn’t show why it mattered. The answer explains that it was a historic first.', w: ['', 'Her life dates don’t show the significance of the performance.', 'This is about a later discovery, not the 1933 performance.', 'This says the performance happened but not why it mattered.'] }
]);
testSet('t2', 'RW', 'm2e', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The baby birds stayed in the nest until their wings were strong enough for them to ______.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['sing', 'fly', 'swim', 'build'], a: 1, x: 'Strong wings let birds **fly**.', w: ['Strong wings aren’t needed to sing.', '', 'Birds don’t need strong wings to swim, and baby birds leave the nest to fly.', 'Strong wings aren’t needed to build.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The ______ crowd cheered loudly when the team scored the winning goal in the final minute.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['enthusiastic', 'silent', 'sleepy', 'absent'], a: 0, x: 'A crowd that cheers loudly is **enthusiastic**.', w: ['', 'A silent crowd doesn’t cheer loudly.', 'A sleepy crowd wouldn’t cheer loudly.', 'An absent crowd isn’t there to cheer.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The new phone’s battery life is ______, lasting two full days on a single charge.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['disappointing', 'average', 'remarkable', 'unknown'], a: 2, x: 'Two full days on one charge is unusually good: **remarkable**.', w: ['Two full days on one charge is impressive, not disappointing.', 'Two full days is better than average.', '', 'The text states how long it lasts, so it isn’t unknown.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The manager asked the team to **address** the customer complaints by Friday so that every customer would receive a response.', p: Q_MEAN('address'), o: ['deal with', 'write on an envelope', 'give a speech to', 'find the location of'], a: 0, x: 'Making sure every customer gets a response means handling, or **dealing with**, the complaints.', w: ['', 'That’s the mail meaning of “address”; you don’t write complaints on envelopes.', 'That’s the speech meaning; the team is responding to complaints, not giving a speech.', 'That’s the location meaning; complaints don’t have an address to find.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 1, ps: 'Sloths move so slowly that algae can grow in their fur, giving them a greenish color. This color helps them blend in with the leaves of the rainforest trees where they live, hiding them from predators such as eagles.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To describe what eagles eat', 'To explain how a sloth’s slowness can help protect it from predators', 'To argue that sloths should be protected by law', 'To compare the speeds of sloths and eagles'], a: 1, x: 'Slowness lets algae grow, which gives camouflage that hides sloths from predators.', w: ['Eagles are mentioned only as predators; their diet isn’t described.', '', 'The text doesn’t mention laws.', 'The text doesn’t compare speeds.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'For centuries, sailors found their way by the stars. Then, in the 1700s, accurate clocks called marine chronometers allowed them to calculate their position at sea much more precisely. Today, most ships rely on satellite systems such as GPS.', p: Q_STRUCT, o: ['It argues that sailors should return to navigating by the stars.', 'It explains how GPS satellites are launched.', 'It traces how methods of navigating at sea have changed over time.', 'It compares two kinds of clocks.'], a: 2, x: 'The text moves from stars, to clocks in the 1700s, to GPS today: a history of navigation.', w: ['The text doesn’t argue for star navigation.', 'GPS is mentioned, but launching satellites isn’t explained.', '', 'Only one kind of clock is mentioned.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 2, ps: '**Text 1**\nZoos protect endangered species by breeding them in safety. Without zoos, many species would already be extinct.\n\n**Text 2**\nZoo breeding programs have helped save some species, such as the California condor. But these successes depend on having protected wild habitat to release animals into. For most endangered species, protecting habitat matters more than breeding animals in captivity.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?', o: ['By arguing that zoos have never helped any endangered species', 'By agreeing that zoos have helped some species but arguing that protecting habitat is usually more important', 'By agreeing that zoos are the most important way to protect endangered species', 'By claiming that the California condor is now extinct'], a: 1, x: 'Text 2 credits zoos with some successes (the condor) but says habitat protection matters more for most species.', w: ['Text 2 credits zoos with helping save the condor.', '', 'Text 2 says habitat protection matters more for most species.', 'Text 2 cites the condor as a success story.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'The Venus flytrap gets most of its energy from sunlight, like other plants. But it grows in soil that is poor in nutrients, so it catches insects to get the nitrogen and other nutrients that the soil lacks.', p: 'According to the text, why does the Venus flytrap catch insects?', o: ['Because it cannot make energy from sunlight', 'To protect itself from being eaten', 'To get nutrients that are missing from the soil where it grows', 'Because insects damage its leaves'], a: 2, x: 'The text says it catches insects to get “nutrients that the soil lacks”.', w: ['The text says it gets most of its energy from sunlight.', 'Protection from being eaten isn’t mentioned.', '', 'Leaf damage isn’t mentioned.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'Long before refrigerators, people found ways to keep food from spoiling. They dried fruit and meat in the sun, packed fish in salt, and pickled vegetables in vinegar. Each method made it harder for the bacteria that cause rotting to grow.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['Salt is the best way to preserve fish.', 'People used several methods to preserve food before refrigerators existed.', 'Refrigerators were invented to replace pickling.', 'Bacteria cannot survive in sunlight.'], a: 1, x: 'The text lists several ways people preserved food before refrigeration.', w: ['Salt is one method; the text doesn’t rank methods.', '', 'The text doesn’t say why refrigerators were invented.', 'Drying in the sun is one method; the text doesn’t claim sunlight kills bacteria.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 1, ps: 'In a short story, the narrator describes her grandfather as generous.', p: 'Which quotation from the story most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“He wore the same brown hat every day.”', '“He never left the market without buying an extra loaf of bread for whoever on the street looked hungry.”', '“He was born in a small village by the sea.”', '“He liked to read the newspaper in the mornings.”'], a: 1, x: 'Buying bread for hungry strangers shows generosity.', w: ['Wearing the same hat doesn’t show generosity.', '', 'His birthplace doesn’t show generosity.', 'Reading the newspaper doesn’t show generosity.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'A student claims that students at her school who eat breakfast do better on morning quizzes than students who don’t.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the student’s claim?', o: ['Most students said they enjoy eating breakfast.', 'The school cafeteria serves breakfast starting at 7:30.', 'Students who ate breakfast and students who skipped it scored about the same on morning quizzes.', 'Students who ate breakfast averaged 85 on morning quizzes, while students who skipped breakfast averaged 74.'], a: 3, x: 'Only one choice compares quiz scores of breakfast eaters and skippers, and it shows the difference the claim predicts.', w: ['Enjoying breakfast doesn’t show anything about quiz scores.', 'The cafeteria’s hours don’t show anything about quiz scores.', 'Equal scores would weaken the claim, not support it.', ''] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 1, ps: 'A city park recorded its average number of daily visitors in each season. The park was busiest in summer: ______', ch: { t: 'bar', title: 'Average daily visitors', x: ['Winter', 'Spring', 'Summer', 'Fall'], s: [{ n: 'Visitors', v: [120, 340, 610, 290] }] }, p: Q_GRAPH, o: ['the park averaged 120 visitors a day in winter.', 'the park averaged 610 visitors a day in summer, more than in any other season.', 'more people visited in spring than in fall.', 'the park averaged 290 visitors a day in fall.'], a: 1, x: 'Only one choice shows that summer had the most visitors.', w: ['This is about winter, not summer.', '', 'This compares spring and fall, not summer.', 'This is about fall, not summer.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 1, ps: 'A farmer noticed that the vegetables growing next to a row of tall trees were smaller than those on the open side of her field, which gets sunlight all day. The trees shade the nearby plants for most of the afternoon. The farmer concluded that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the vegetables near the trees grew less because they received less sunlight.', 'the trees were stealing the vegetables.', 'vegetables grow best in the shade.', 'the open side of the field needs more water.'], a: 0, x: 'The only difference described is shade, so less sunlight explains the smaller vegetables.', w: ['', 'Trees don’t steal vegetables.', 'The vegetables in the shade were smaller, so shade didn’t help.', 'Water isn’t mentioned; the difference is shade.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'Some bird species that live on islands without predators have lost the ability to fly. Flying uses a lot of energy, and on these islands birds can find food on the ground without ever needing to escape from danger. This suggests that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['all island birds are unable to fly.', 'flying helps birds find more food.', 'when birds don’t need flight to escape predators, they may gradually lose the ability to fly.', 'birds on islands use more energy than other birds.'], a: 2, x: 'Without predators, flight costs energy without a benefit, so it can be lost over time. “All island birds” goes too far.', w: ['The text says some island birds, not all of them.', 'The text says birds on these islands find food on the ground without flying.', '', 'The text says flying uses energy; it doesn’t compare island birds’ energy use with other birds’.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'Our class planted a vegetable garden this ______ will harvest the tomatoes in August.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['spring, we', 'spring; we', 'spring we', 'spring, and, we'], a: 1, x: 'Two complete sentences are joined, so a semicolon is needed. A comma alone is a comma splice.', w: ['A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', '', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'Last year, the city planted more than 500 trees along its ______ even more are planned for next year.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['streets, and', 'streets, and,', 'streets and,', 'streets, so,'], a: 0, x: 'A comma plus “and” joins two complete sentences. No comma goes after the conjunction.', w: ['', 'No comma belongs after “and”.', 'The comma belongs before “and”, not after it.', 'No comma belongs after “so”, and “so” would wrongly make the new plans a result of the planting.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'Jazz musician Louis Armstrong, who grew up in New ______ became famous for his trumpet playing and his gravelly singing voice.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['Orleans', 'Orleans;', 'Orleans:', 'Orleans,'], a: 3, x: 'The “who” clause is opened with a comma, so it must also be closed with one before the verb “became”.', w: ['The “who” clause opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma before “became”.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “became”.', 'A colon would cut the subject off from its verb, “became”.', ''] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The tour guide asked the visitors whether they had ever seen a glacier ______', p: Q_SEC, o: ['before?', 'before.', 'before!', 'before;'], a: 1, x: 'The sentence **reports** a question (“asked… whether”) rather than asking one, so it ends with a period.', w: ['The sentence reports a question rather than asking one, so it ends with a period.', '', 'Nothing in the sentence calls for an exclamation.', 'A semicolon can’t end a sentence.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'My grandmother and I ______ to the farmers market together every Saturday morning.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['goes', 'is going', 'go', 'has gone'], a: 2, x: '“My grandmother and I” is a plural subject, so the verb is “go”.', w: ['“My grandmother and I” is plural, so the verb must be plural.', '“Is going” is singular, but “my grandmother and I” is plural.', '', '“Has gone” is singular, and the sentence describes a regular habit.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'The old ______ blades were damaged in the storm, so the farmer repaired it before spring.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['windmills', 'windmill’s', 'windmills’', 'windmills’s'], a: 1, x: '“Repaired **it**” shows there is one windmill, and the blades belong to it: “windmill’s”.', w: ['“Windmills” has no apostrophe, so it doesn’t show the blades belong to it.', '', '“Windmills’” means several windmills, but “repaired it” shows there is one.', '“Windmills’s” isn’t a correct form, and there is only one windmill.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'By the time the rescue team arrived, the stranded hikers ______ a shelter out of branches.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['had built', 'build', 'will build', 'are building'], a: 0, x: 'The building happened before the team arrived (in the past), so the past perfect “had built” fits.', w: ['', '“Build” is present tense, but the team arrived in the past.', '“Will build” is future tense, but the team already arrived.', '“Are building” is present tense, but the sentence is about the past.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'Cheetahs are the fastest land animals. ______ they can run at top speed for only a short distance before they must rest.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['For example,', 'Therefore,', 'In addition,', 'However,'], a: 3, x: 'Being fastest contrasts with being able to keep it up only briefly.', w: ['Running only a short distance isn’t an example of being fastest; it’s a limitation.', 'Tiring quickly isn’t a result of being the fastest in this text.', '“In addition” adds a similar point, but this one is a limitation.', ''] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'The recipe is very simple. ______ it requires only four ingredients and takes ten minutes to make.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['In fact,', 'However,', 'Instead,', 'On the other hand,'], a: 0, x: 'The second sentence backs up and expands on “very simple”: “In fact”.', w: ['', 'Four ingredients and ten minutes support “very simple”; nothing contrasts.', '“Instead” signals a replacement, but the details support the first sentence.', 'The details back up the first sentence rather than offering another side.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'The museum closed for repairs in March, and workers began fixing the roof. ______ the staff used the time to reorganize the exhibits.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['In contrast,', 'For example,', 'Meanwhile,', 'As a result,'], a: 2, x: 'The staff’s work happened during the same period as the repairs: “Meanwhile”.', w: ['Reorganizing exhibits doesn’t contrast with fixing the roof; both happened during the closure.', 'Reorganizing exhibits isn’t an example of fixing the roof.', '', 'Reorganizing wasn’t caused by the roof repairs; it happened at the same time.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- The Komodo dragon is the largest living lizard.\n- It can grow up to 3 meters long.\n- It lives on a few islands in Indonesia.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize the size of the Komodo dragon. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The Komodo dragon lives on a few islands in Indonesia.', 'The Komodo dragon, the largest living lizard, can grow up to 3 meters long.', 'The Komodo dragon is a lizard.', 'Komodo dragons live in Indonesia, a country with many islands.'], a: 1, x: 'Only one choice is about size.', w: ['This gives where it lives, not its size.', '', 'This doesn’t say anything about size.', 'This is about Indonesia, not the dragon’s size.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Mount Everest is the highest mountain above sea level, at about 8,849 meters.\n- Mauna Kea, in Hawaii, rises about 4,207 meters above sea level.\n- Most of Mauna Kea is underwater.\n- Measured from its base on the ocean floor, Mauna Kea is more than 10,000 meters tall.', p: 'The student wants to explain why some people consider Mauna Kea taller than Mount Everest. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Mount Everest is about 8,849 meters high.', 'Mauna Kea rises about 4,207 meters above sea level.', 'Measured from its base on the ocean floor, Mauna Kea is more than 10,000 meters tall, while Everest rises about 8,849 meters above sea level.', 'Mauna Kea is a mountain in Hawaii.'], a: 2, x: 'The explanation needs Mauna Kea’s full height from its base compared with Everest’s height.', w: ['This gives only Everest’s height, so it doesn’t explain why Mauna Kea could be called taller.', 'Above sea level, Mauna Kea is much shorter, so this doesn’t explain the claim.', '', 'This gives the location, not its height.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.\n- Antarctica is the largest desert overall.\n- A desert is defined by how little precipitation it receives, not by its temperature.', p: 'The student wants to explain why Antarctica is considered a desert. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.', 'Antarctica is the largest desert overall.', 'Both the Sahara and Antarctica are deserts.', 'Antarctica is considered a desert because deserts are defined by low precipitation, not by temperature.'], a: 3, x: 'Only one choice gives the reason: the definition of a desert depends on precipitation.', w: ['This is about the Sahara, not Antarctica.', 'This says Antarctica is a desert but not why.', 'This says both are deserts but not why Antarctica counts.', ''] }
]);
testSet('t2', 'RW', 'm2h', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The diplomat’s reply was deliberately ______, allowing both sides to read it as support for their own position.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['emphatic', 'equivocal', 'hostile', 'candid'], a: 1, x: 'A reply both sides can interpret their own way is open to more than one meaning: **equivocal**.', w: ['An emphatic reply would be forceful and clear, not open to both sides’ readings.', '', 'A hostile reply wouldn’t let both sides read it as support.', 'A candid reply would be frank and clear, not open to different readings.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Though the scientist’s early papers were largely ______ by her peers, her ideas were vindicated decades later, when new techniques confirmed her findings.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['celebrated', 'funded', 'overlooked', 'plagiarized'], a: 2, x: '“Though” sets up a contrast with being vindicated later, so her ideas were at first ignored: **overlooked**.', w: ['“Though” sets up a contrast with being vindicated later, so her papers weren’t celebrated at first.', 'Funding doesn’t contrast with being vindicated later.', '', 'Nothing suggests others copied her work; the point is that it was ignored at first.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The committee’s report offered only **qualified** praise for the program: it applauded the program’s goals while raising serious concerns about its cost.', p: Q_MEAN('qualified'), o: ['skilled', 'certified', 'eligible', 'limited'], a: 3, x: 'Praise mixed with serious concerns is restricted, or **limited**, praise.', w: ['“Skilled” is the meaning for a person (“a qualified nurse”), not for praise.', '“Certified” is the meaning for a person, not for praise.', '“Eligible” is the meaning for a person, not for praise.', ''] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The historian cautions against ______ the past, noting that nostalgic accounts of earlier centuries tend to leave out disease, hunger and hard labor.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['documenting', 'romanticizing', 'condemning', 'investigating'], a: 1, x: 'Leaving out the hardships makes the past look better than it was: **romanticizing** it.', w: ['Recording the past accurately is what the historian wants, not something to caution against.', '', 'Leaving out hardships makes the past look better, not worse, so it isn’t condemning.', 'Investigating the past isn’t the problem the historian describes.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'An essayist argues that cities should plant more street trees. ==Critics often object that trees are expensive to maintain, but a study of five U.S. cities found that the benefits of street trees, such as lower energy costs and cleaner air, outweighed their costs in every city studied.== For this reason, the essayist concludes, trees are an investment rather than an expense.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It states the main claim that the rest of the text supports.', 'It describes a weakness in the study’s methods.', 'It acknowledges a likely objection to the essayist’s argument and presents evidence against it.', 'It introduces a topic unrelated to the essayist’s argument.'], a: 2, x: 'The sentence raises the cost objection and answers it with the study. The main claim was already stated in the first sentence.', w: ['The main claim (cities should plant more trees) is in the first sentence.', 'The sentence uses the study as evidence; it doesn’t criticize its methods.', '', 'The sentence is directly about the costs and benefits of trees, the essay’s topic.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'Every morning, Mr. Hale arranged the apples in his shop window into a perfect pyramid. Every afternoon, the children from the school across the street pulled one from the bottom and ran off, laughing, as the pyramid collapsed. And every morning, Mr. Hale built it again, though he had begun stacking it a little closer to the door, where small hands could reach it more easily.', p: 'Which choice best describes the function of the last sentence in the text as a whole?', o: ['It shows that Mr. Hale is trying to stop the children from taking apples.', 'It explains how Mr. Hale first became a shopkeeper.', 'It reveals that Mr. Hale may welcome the children’s game rather than resent it.', 'It suggests that Mr. Hale has stopped selling apples.'], a: 2, x: 'Moving the pyramid where children can reach it more easily shows he’s quietly joining in, not trying to stop them.', w: ['Moving the pyramid closer to where children can reach it makes the game easier, not harder.', 'The text doesn’t explain how he became a shopkeeper.', '', 'He rebuilds the apple pyramid every morning, so he still sells apples.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nSome urban planners argue that adding lanes to highways is the best way to reduce traffic congestion: more lanes mean more room for cars.\n\n**Text 2**\nStudies of U.S. cities have found that when highway capacity grows, the total distance people drive tends to grow by about the same proportion within a few years, as people make trips they previously avoided. Researchers call this effect “induced demand.”', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the urban planners’ argument in Text 1?', o: ['By agreeing that more lanes permanently reduce congestion', 'By claiming that people drive less after highways are widened', 'By arguing that adding lanes may not reduce congestion for long, because the extra room tends to attract more driving', 'By arguing that highways should be built only in rural areas'], a: 2, x: 'Induced demand means the new space fills up with new trips, so congestion returns.', w: ['Text 2 says driving grows to fill the new space, so the benefit doesn’t last.', 'Text 2 says people drive more after highways grow.', '', 'Text 2 doesn’t mention rural areas.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 3, ps: 'Economists generally observe that raising the price of a product reduces how much of it people buy. For certain luxury goods, however, such as designer handbags, a higher price can make the item more desirable, because buyers value the status that comes from owning something expensive. Such goods appear to be exceptions to the usual pattern.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['Designer handbags are more expensive than other handbags.', 'People never buy less of a product when its price rises.', 'Certain luxury goods may be exceptions to the general pattern that higher prices reduce demand.', 'Economists have stopped studying how prices affect demand.'], a: 2, x: 'The text states a general rule and then describes luxury goods as an exception to it.', w: ['Handbags are an example; the main idea is about price and demand.', 'The text says people generally buy less when prices rise.', '', 'The text describes what economists observe; it doesn’t say they stopped studying it.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 3, ps: 'When the letter from the university arrived, Tomás left it unopened on the kitchen table for three days. He walked past it on his way to work and on his way home, and once he picked it up and held it to the light before setting it down again exactly where it had been.', p: 'Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about Tomás?', o: ['He has forgotten that the letter is there.', 'He is anxious about what the letter might say.', 'He already knows the letter contains good news.', 'He has decided not to attend the university.'], a: 1, x: 'He clearly knows the letter is there (he picks it up) but can’t bring himself to open it, which suggests anxiety.', w: ['He walks past it and even picks it up, so he hasn’t forgotten it.', '', 'If he knew the news was good, he would have no reason to avoid opening it.', 'Nothing shows he has decided anything; he hasn’t even opened the letter.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'Researchers hypothesized that bumblebees learn which flowers to visit partly by watching other bees.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers’ hypothesis?', o: ['Bumblebees visit many different kinds of flowers.', 'Bumblebees that had not watched other bees chose blue and yellow flowers equally often.', 'Bumblebees that watched trained bees feed from blue flowers later chose blue flowers more often than bees that had not watched, even when no reward was offered.', 'Some bumblebee colonies contain hundreds of bees.'], a: 2, x: 'Only one choice compares bees that watched with bees that didn’t and shows watching changed their choices. Bees that hadn’t watched choosing both colors equally is just a baseline; on its own it shows nothing about watching.', w: ['Visiting many flowers says nothing about learning by watching.', 'This is only a baseline; on its own it shows nothing about the effect of watching.', '', 'Colony size says nothing about learning by watching.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'An art historian claims that a newly discovered painting was made by the 17th-century Dutch artist Judith Leyster.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the art historian’s claim?', o: ['The painting’s style resembles that of Leyster’s known works.', 'Chemical analysis shows that the painting contains a blue pigment that was not invented until the 1800s.', 'Leyster was one of the few women admitted to the painters’ guild in her city.', 'The painting was found in a collection that includes several other Dutch works from the 1600s.'], a: 1, x: 'A pigment invented in the 1800s couldn’t appear in a painting made in the 1600s, so the claim would be very unlikely.', w: ['A similar style would support the claim, not weaken it.', '', 'Leyster’s guild membership doesn’t say anything about this painting.', 'Being found with other 1600s Dutch works fits the claim.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 3, ps: 'A city began charging households for each bag of trash they put out. The city claims that the program reduced the amount of trash without simply causing people to dump trash illegally.', ch: { t: 'table', title: 'Before and after the program', head: ['Year', 'Trash collected (tons)', 'Recycling collected (tons)', 'Illegal dumping reports'], rows: [['2021 (before)', '52,000', '18,000', '140'], ['2022 (after)', '41,000', '26,000', '145']] }, p: 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support the city’s claim?', o: ['Recycling collected rose from 18,000 tons to 26,000 tons.', 'Reports of illegal dumping rose from 140 to 145.', 'Trash collected fell from 52,000 tons to 41,000 tons, while reports of illegal dumping stayed nearly the same.', 'Trash and recycling together totaled 70,000 tons in 2021.'], a: 2, x: 'The claim has two parts: less trash, and no jump in illegal dumping. Only one choice addresses both.', w: ['More recycling doesn’t show what happened to trash or illegal dumping.', 'This shows only that dumping stayed nearly the same, not that trash fell.', '', 'This describes only the year before the program.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'Astronomers noticed that a distant star dims slightly every 12 days, and each dip lasts about three hours. The dips are always the same size. One explanation is that a planet passes in front of the star on a regular orbit. If this explanation is correct, then ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the dips should continue to occur about every 12 days.', 'the star must be getting permanently dimmer.', 'the planet must be larger than the star.', 'the dips should get longer each time.'], a: 0, x: 'A planet on a regular orbit would pass in front of the star on the same schedule, so the dips should keep repeating every 12 days.', w: ['', 'The dips are temporary and repeat; nothing suggests permanent dimming.', 'The dips are slight, which suggests a planet much smaller than the star.', 'A regular orbit would make dips of the same length, which the text says they are.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'Medieval European books were usually written on parchment made from animal skin, which was expensive. Scribes sometimes scraped the ink off old books so that the parchment could be reused for new texts. Modern imaging techniques can reveal faint traces of the scraped-off writing. This suggests that some reused books ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['were written on paper rather than parchment.', 'can no longer be read by any method.', 'were scraped clean because no one valued the original texts.', 'may preserve older texts that would otherwise be unknown today.'], a: 3, x: 'If the erased writing can be recovered, these books may hold texts that survive nowhere else.', w: ['The text says the books were written on parchment.', 'Modern imaging can reveal the scraped-off writing.', 'Scribes reused parchment because it was expensive, not necessarily because the texts were unwanted.', ''] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The reef’s most surprising ______ a fish that changes from female to male as it ages—has long fascinated biologists.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['resident,', 'resident—', 'resident', 'resident;'], a: 1, x: 'The description ends with a dash before “has”, so it must also begin with a dash.', w: ['The description ends with a dash, so it must begin with a dash, not a comma.', '', 'Without opening punctuation, the description runs into “resident”.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “has fascinated”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'Researchers found something unexpected in the ice ______ microbes that had survived, dormant, for thousands of years.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['core;', 'core, these', 'core:', 'core, such as'], a: 2, x: 'A complete sentence comes before the blank and what follows explains the “something unexpected”, so a colon fits. A semicolon would need a complete sentence after it.', w: ['A semicolon needs a complete sentence after it, but “microbes that had survived…” isn’t one.', '“These microbes had survived…” would be a complete sentence, so the comma creates a comma splice.', '', '“Such as” would mean the microbes were only one example, but they are the unexpected thing itself.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The city’s oldest bridge, built in ______ still carries thousands of cars each day.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['1874', '1874,', '1874;', '1874—'], a: 1, x: '“Built in 1874” is set off by commas; the opening comma needs a closing one.', w: ['The phrase opens with a comma after “bridge”, so it needs a closing comma.', '', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “carries”.', 'The phrase opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma, not a dash.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'Some metals conduct electricity extremely ______ silver, for instance, conducts it better than any other metal.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['well,', 'well', 'well;', 'well, and'], a: 2, x: '“Silver, for instance, conducts it…” is a complete sentence, so a semicolon is needed. “For instance” sits inside the second sentence and doesn’t change that.', w: ['A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', '', '“And” with “for instance” makes the sentence awkward; the second part is an example, which a semicolon introduces cleanly.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'The number of students who apply to the summer program ______ risen every year since 2015.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['have', 'has', 'are', 'were'], a: 1, x: 'The subject is “the number” (singular), not “students”, so “has”.', w: ['The subject is “the number”, which is singular; “students” is part of a phrase.', '', '“Are” is plural, and “are risen” isn’t a correct verb form.', '“Were” is plural, and “were risen” isn’t a correct verb form.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'Each of the nations at the climate summit presented ______ own plan for reducing emissions.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['their', 'its', 'it’s', 'they’re'], a: 1, x: '“Each” is singular and refers to a nation, so “its”. “It’s” means “it is”.', w: ['“Each” is singular, so “their” doesn’t agree.', '', '“It’s” means “it is”.', '“They’re” means “they are”.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'A former professional dancer, ______', p: Q_SEC, o: ['Ms. Ortiz’s classes are popular with students of all ages.', 'students of all ages enjoy Ms. Ortiz’s classes.', 'the classes taught by Ms. Ortiz are popular with students of all ages.', 'Ms. Ortiz teaches classes that are popular with students of all ages.'], a: 3, x: 'The former dancer is Ms. Ortiz herself, so “Ms. Ortiz” must come right after the comma. “Ms. Ortiz’s classes” puts the classes there.', w: ['This puts Ms. Ortiz’s classes right after the comma, making it sound like the classes were a dancer.', 'This makes it sound like the students were a former dancer.', 'This makes it sound like the classes were a former dancer.', ''] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Some scientists predicted that the new vaccine would need to be stored at extremely low temperatures. ______ tests showed it stayed stable for weeks in an ordinary refrigerator, making it far easier to distribute.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Likewise,', 'As it turned out,', 'Specifically,', 'In addition,'], a: 1, x: 'The tests revealed something different from the prediction: “As it turned out”.', w: ['The test results differ from the prediction, so they aren’t similar.', '', 'The test results don’t give details of the prediction; they contradict it.', 'The test results don’t add to the prediction; they contradict it.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Diamond is the hardest natural material, resisting scratches from almost anything. ______ it is surprisingly brittle: a sharp blow at the right angle can split a diamond in two.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Consequently,', 'Similarly,', 'In fact,', 'Nevertheless,'], a: 3, x: 'Hard to scratch, yet easy to split: the second fact contrasts with the first.', w: ['Brittleness isn’t a result of hardness; the text treats it as surprising.', 'Being brittle isn’t similar to resisting scratches.', '“In fact” would support or strengthen the first sentence, but this sentence contrasts with it.', ''] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'The new theory explained existing experimental results well. ______ it made a prediction that no one had thought to test: that light would bend as it passed near the Sun.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['However,', 'Instead,', 'Even so,', 'Moreover,'], a: 3, x: 'The prediction is a further point in the theory’s favor, added to the first: “Moreover”.', w: ['The prediction adds to the theory’s strengths; nothing contrasts.', '“Instead” would mean the theory did something other than explain results, but it did both.', '“Even so” signals a contrast, but the prediction is another point in the theory’s favor.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- Many animals use camouflage to avoid being seen.\n- The Arctic fox’s coat turns white in winter, matching the snow.\n- The leaf-tailed gecko’s flat, ragged body looks like a dead leaf.\n- The cuttlefish can change the color of its skin in less than a second to match its background.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize that animals achieve camouflage in different ways. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The Arctic fox’s coat turns white in winter, matching the snow.', 'Many animals use camouflage to avoid being seen.', 'Both the Arctic fox and the cuttlefish can change color.', 'Animals camouflage themselves in different ways: the Arctic fox’s coat changes with the seasons, the leaf-tailed gecko is shaped like a dead leaf, and the cuttlefish changes its skin color in an instant.'], a: 3, x: 'Only one choice shows several different methods. Saying the fox and the cuttlefish can both change color points out a similarity, not variety.', w: ['This gives only one animal, so it doesn’t show different ways.', 'This is a general statement without examples of different methods.', 'This points out a similarity, not different ways.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- The Svalbard Global Seed Vault is on a remote Norwegian island in the Arctic.\n- It stores backup copies of seeds from seed banks around the world.\n- It is built deep inside a mountain, where frozen ground keeps the seeds cold even without power.\n- If crops are lost to disaster or disease, the stored seeds can be used to replant them.', p: 'The student wants to explain the purpose of the vault to an audience unfamiliar with it. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The vault is built deep inside a mountain, where frozen ground keeps the seeds cold.', 'The Svalbard Global Seed Vault is on a remote Norwegian island in the Arctic.', 'It stores backup copies of seeds so that lost crops can be replanted.', 'The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, on a remote Norwegian island, stores backup copies of seeds from around the world so that crops lost to disaster or disease can be replanted.'], a: 3, x: 'The goal is the purpose, for readers who don’t know the vault. The sentence starting “It stores backup copies…” gives the purpose but never says what “it” is.', w: ['This describes how the vault keeps seeds cold, not its purpose.', 'This gives the location, not the purpose.', 'This gives the purpose but never says what “it” is, which an unfamiliar audience needs.', ''] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- In 1928, a plane called the *Southern Cross* flew from California to Australia.\n- The flight was led by the Australian pilot Charles Kingsford Smith, with a crew of four.\n- It took about nine days, with stops in Hawaii and Fiji.\n- No one had flown across the Pacific Ocean before.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize the historical importance of the flight. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The flight took about nine days, with stops in Hawaii and Fiji.', 'Charles Kingsford Smith led a crew of four.', 'In 1928, a crew led by Charles Kingsford Smith completed the first flight across the Pacific Ocean.', 'The *Southern Cross* flew from California to Australia.'], a: 2, x: 'Historical importance means it was a first: the first flight across the Pacific.', w: ['Travel details don’t show why the flight mattered.', 'The crew size doesn’t show historical importance.', '', 'This describes the route but doesn’t say it was the first.'] }
]);
